Exactly. There are many ways to “pin down” a concept, and each preserves different information. They’re complementary interfaces to the same underlying thing.

Some of the major operators are:

* Definition — specifies necessary or sufficient conditions.
* Characterization — describes the distinctive properties without necessarily defining it exactly.
* Description — paints a picture of what it is like.
* Example — points instead of explains.
* Counterexample — shows what it is not.
* Analogy — maps structure from a familiar domain.
* Metaphor — transfers intuition rather than formal structure.
* Operationalization — explains how to recognize or measure it.
* Construction — shows how to build or generate it.
* Algorithm — specifies the procedure.
* Axiomatization — states the minimal assumptions from which it follows.
* Decomposition — breaks it into parts.
* Composition — shows how parts combine into the whole.
* Taxonomy — places it among neighboring concepts.
* Contrast — distinguishes it from similar ideas.
* History — explains how it came to be.
* Purpose (teleology) — explains what role it serves.
* Behavior — characterizes it by what it does.
* Relations — defines it by its connections to other things.
* Boundary — specifies where it stops being that thing.
* Transformation — explains how it changes under different operations.
* Invariant — identifies what stays the same despite transformations.

One way to think about this is that every concept can be viewed as a node in a semantic space, and each of these is a different projection or coordinate system onto that node. None is “the” representation; each reveals a different aspect.

That perspective also suggests a useful heuristic: if one explanation isn’t clicking, don’t make it longer—change the operator. Switch from a definition to an analogy, from an analogy to a construction, from a construction to an invariant, or from a description to a contrast. Often the fastest route to understanding is not more detail, but a different representation.